- H350722 (16 Jan 2026) confirmed by law-firm commentary (Diaz Trade Law, Barnes Richardson, GingerControl): unlicensed platform doing OCR of entry data and Form 5106 filing = customs business; AI HTS suggestions OK ONLY if no nexus to actual importation (earlier H290535 2022 was impermissible). https://diaztradelaw.com/even-ai-needs-a-license-know-when-automation-unlawfully-crosses-into-customs-business/ ; https://www.barnesrichardson.com/cbp-ruling-clarifies-customs-business-addresses-ai ; https://gingercontrol.com/blog/cbp-ai-ruling-h350722-trade-compliance (vendor, interested). Ruling text not fetched (CROSS).
- Perfectus FCA settlement $549.5M announced 12 May 2026 (AD/CVD evasion on aluminum extrusions, "pallets" scheme); Ceratizit $54.4M Dec 2025 prior record. https://www.morganlewis.com/pubs/2026/05/doj-announces-major-fca-settlement-relating-to-evaded-customs-duties ; https://www.afslaw.com/perspectives/investigations-blog/doj-secures-record-550-million-settlement-over-alleged-aluminum . NB Perfectus was AD/CVD and 2011-14 conduct, NOT 301 origin; idea's framing ("converting origin errors") is loose.
- Altana: $1B valuation Series C Aug 2024 ($200M), ~$322M raised; CBP selected Product Passports (Oct 2025 release; GBI program via CTPAT importers, passport ID on entries); acquired Cervo AI Jul 2026 (agentic entry writer for logistics providers; deal could exceed $100M); customer base 8 of 10 largest logistics providers. https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20251001718752/en/U.S.-Customs-and-Border-Protection-Selects-Altanas-AI-Powered-Product-Passports-to-Drive-Next-Generation-Supply-Chain-Traceability-Trusted-Trade ; https://www.01net.it/altana-acquires-cervo-ai-to-deliver-end-to-end-agentic-ai-platform-for-customs-brokerage/ ; https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/supply-chain-startup-unicorn-altana
- CBP CF-28 volume up since Sep 2025 (vendor blog, interpretive): https://www.gingercontrol.com/blog/cf-28-audits-spiking-2026-trend . CBP ANPRM on supply-chain visibility could require foreign export docs/cert of origin (BRG): https://www.thinkbrg.com/insights/publications/cbp-seeks-input-on-potential-new-import-documentation-and-supply-chain-transparency-requirements/ . Tailwind but unfinished.
- Melt/smelt documentation is mill test certs / supplier attestation / BOL; existing: Assent (supplier data), TIC giants SGS/Intertek/BV (not confirmed to sell this), forensic origin firms.
- Analogy supporting factory-paid model: TIC (SGS, Intertek, BV) is a $100B+ factory-paid industry; supports scale in principle but those incumbents own audits.
- Prelim verdict: WEAK/near kill. median ~$5M, upside ~$40M. Per-shipment $20 attestation with only self-attestation is thin; real attestation needs audits ($1-5k) so the unit economics collide; counsel-privilege vs discoverable vault; Altana owns CBP-facing side.

## Idea 4 Licence Bank (searches done: 5)
- Roll-up already exists: Alba Wheels Up (Southfield Capital platform 2021; add-ons KSI Nov 2022, V.T. Mancusi Jul 2023, John A. Steer Jan 2024, Perishable Specialist May 2025; offices CA FL MD NJ PA VA). https://www.southfieldcapital.com/news-article/alba-wheels-up-and-southfield-capital-announce-thestrategic-acquisition-of-john-a-steer-co ; Livingston/Platinum Equity (2019); Flexport Customs. 
- AI-native entrants: Amari AI ($4.5M seed Feb 2026, Pear+First Round, 30+ customers, >$15B goods moved; sells to brokerages) https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/19/this-former-big-tech-engineers-are-using-ai-to-navigate-trumps-trade-chaos/ ; Alchemize (YC S26, 2 founders, ~$500k pre-seed, AI-native brokerage) https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/alchemize ; Altana+Cervo AI (21 Jul 2026, "end-to-end agentic AI platform for customs brokerage", deal could exceed $100M) https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260721109771/en/ . 
- Market: Mordor US customs brokerage $5.48B 2026 (paid summary, secondary); CBP ~14,454 active licensed individuals (cbp.gov/node/77830); firm count unverified (6,200 is one blog). Pricing $100-250/entry full-service. KGH (Maersk bought, SEK 2.6B, 16.3x EBITDA, 18% EBITDA margin) = strategic premium comp; small-firm asking prices ~1.5x revenue / 3.9x SDE (listings only). 
- Premise doubts: "licence is the cap" overstated. A corporate broker licence needs one licensed officer + responsible supervision; national permits let Flexport scale; the real bottleneck is entry-writer labour, which AI removes for ALL brokers, not just the acquirer. Scarcity moat is weak (14k licensees; hireable). 
- Idea falls on round-1 cliche list ("AI roll-up"); revenue is bought; needs $50-100M capital; funded competitors already doing both halves (Alba = roll-up, Amari/Cervo = AI stack).
- Prelim verdict: WEAK. median ~$18M, upside ~$90M (needs capital raise + AI-margin expansion + licensed chassis sales to vendors).

## Idea 5 Second Filer (searches done: 3 + shared)
- Contingent post-entry recovery is CROWDED: Tarifflo (YC S26; licensed-broker ex-KPMG founder; contingency "fee only on refund"; claims 1 in 8 of 1M+ entries overpaid; $1.3M recovered for one beauty importer; also SaaS $2.5k-75k/yr) https://yespress.io/tarifflo-yc-s26 ; Duty Discovery = FRS + ProfitTrust, no upfront cost, paid after refund https://www.yamatoamerica.com/solutions/duty-refund-service/ (Yamato America duty refund service, same genre) ; Leyton (global contingent recovery consultancy, US duty recovery "risk-free") https://leyton.com/us/insights/articles/duty-recovery-for-u-s-importers-unlock-hidden-customs-savings ; Baker Tilly tariff refund recovery https://www.bakertilly.com/services/tariff-refund-recovery-services ; KlearNow post-entry audit case $1.7M https://klearnow.ai/case-studies/post-entry-audit-finds-1-7m-in-duty-overcharges-hidden-by-tariff-stacking/ ; GingerControl audits/PSC/protest guide https://gingercontrol.com/blog/recover-overpaid-import-duties
- Big Four ban premise TRUE but not a gap: SEC Rule 2-01(c)(5) bars contingent fees for audit clients (no materiality test; EY/Cintas 2021 enforcement) https://www.sec.gov/oca/aapwc ; but non-audit-firm entrants already take contingency (Leyton, Duty Discovery, Tarifflo, Yamato). "No conflict-free contingent entrant" is FALSE.
- Math: windows. PSC ~300 days, protest 180d post-liquidation, 1520(d) 1 yr. So only ~1-1.5 years of entries are fixable, not "five years of ACE data" (only drawback reaches 5 yrs). The 0.6% overpay rate rests on Tarifflo's self-reported 1-in-8 x idea's own 5% assumption; PR figure "$5.8B/yr overpaid" appears in Duty Discovery press release (so ~2.7% of $214B duty; fee pool at 30% = $1.7B on that claim if all captured; at 0.6% = $385M).
- Prelim verdict: KILL/WEAK (differentiator false + crowded). median ~$7M, upside ~$45M.

## Idea 6 Peer Ledger (searches done: 3)
- No product found that pools declared entry data for peer-outlier scoring (arXiv 2412.14179 benchmarks classification TOOLS only). So a real gap, but real reasons: antitrust (information exchange among competitors on values), discovery/privilege of a shared "reasonable care" file, importers' unwillingness to pool adverse facts, cold start.
- Surety/lender buyers: customs bond market is small-ticket (std $50k bond premium ~$400-600/yr); CBP bond insufficiencies FY2025 27,479 / ~$3.6B (secondary, unconfirmed): https://www.trucordia.com/blog/how-tariff-changes-are-driving-up-u.s.-customs-bond-requirements ; Foley Jun 2026 on rising bond/collateral requirements https://www.foley.com/insights/publications/2026/06/what-every-multinational-should-know-about-the-new-customs-enforcement-realities-part-i-managing-rising-bond-and-collateral-requirements/ . Institutional buyers (customs sureties) number in the tens, not 300; $150k x 300 = $45M is not credible.
- Importer base: Census 242,515 importers 2023; no size-class data found; the pitch's "40,000 importers with $250k+ duty" is unsourced and likely 3-5x high (my estimate 8-12k on a ~$5M-of-imports/5% rate cut-off; unverified). 11,000 x $5k needs 27% of the idea's own base.
- Substitutes: ACE Reports (free), CBP rulings CROSS (free), classification tools, ISA program, broker QA.
- Prelim verdict: KILL. median ~$3M, upside ~$30M.
